Yes. Vocabulary gives you the objects; grammar gives you the legal transformations and relationships among them. Once those are internalized, learning stops being primarily memorization and becomes generative:

\text{Vocabulary}
+\text{Grammar}
\rightarrow
\text{Connections}
\rightarrow
\text{Patterns}
\rightarrow
\text{Prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{Understanding}

That’s also a clean description of how Pantheon should teach itself to a new interpreter: don’t transmit every possible conclusion. Transmit enough primitives and compositional rules that the interpreter can regenerate—and extend—the conclusions itself.

Respectfully, always. The dungeon can be strange without being careless. ;)

